    "explanation": "The charge turns on one number: how long the driver held the pickup. Both parties agree the driver reached the pin and tapped 'I'm Here' at 22:38:37, and that he terminated the trip at 22:41:36. POL-NS-01(c) requires a continuous in-geofence dwell of at least the free waiting period, measured from the first qualifying in-geofence GPS fix and not from the arrival tap. The free period in force is read from the record: trip_record.platform_policy.free_wait_sec = 180 seconds. The platform's own GPS trace measures a single continuous in-geofence run of 179 seconds (19 qualifying fixes, 22:38:37 to 22:41:36, closest fix 0.31 m from the pin). That is one second short of 180. POL-NS-01 limb (c) therefore fails, and limb (d) fails with it: 180 seconds from 22:38:37 elapses at 22:41:37, and the driver cancelled at 22:41:36. Because the fee is not chargeable under POL-NS-01, POL-NS-06 governs - where the effective in-geofence dwell is shorter than the free waiting period, the cancellation fee is not chargeable and is refunded in full, whether or not the rider was present and whether or not the rider responded. To the driver: your point that the arrival marker was true, that you were stationary on the pin the whole time and that the trace runs on to 22:42:46, is accepted - POL-NS-02 is satisfied (19 consecutive accuracy-qualifying fixes inside the 50 m STANDARD radius) and POL-NS-05 does not fire (0.0 km/h at the pin at 22:38:37). But POL-NS-02 decides arrival, not dwell. Your argument that the one-second gap is a boundary effect of the 10-second export cadence is understood and is factually plausible, but POL-NS-06 is deliberately strict liability - the policy states that even a rider who was genuinely absent gets the fee back where the driver cancelled a few seconds early - and there is no de minimis allowance anywhere in the category. The shortfall also cannot be bridged: POL-NS-06(i) permits separate in-geofence intervals to be summed only against a present, populated traffic_advisory, and EV-ADVIS-02717-01 is MISSING (weight 0.0, issue MISSING_ARTIFACT), while the trace shows a single interval (run_count 1). POL-NS-07 accordingly pays you nothing: it applies only 'where POL-NS-01 is satisfied in full and no clause from POL-NS-03 to POL-NS-06 displaces it', and here POL-NS-01 fails and POL-NS-06 displaces it, so the 3.60 SGD payout is not owed; nor does the anti-farming check arise (no_show_terminations_30d 2, below the 4-limb, rate 0.022, below 0.08). To the rider: your account that the driver 'cancelled almost immediately' is not what the record shows - he was en route 8.3 minutes and waited 2 minutes 59 seconds - and POL-NS-04 does not exonerate you (you sent no message, and your device track sits about 900 m from the pin). You succeed on the dwell arithmetic alone, which POL-NS-06 makes decisive regardless of rider presence. Integrity: the fare ledger's cancellation-fee line of 4.50 reconciles exactly with the platform fee of 4.50, so no POL-NS-08(d) defect; the trace is COMPLETE at weight 1.0 with 100% coverage and no gaps, and the chat log matches trip_record.chat_message_count, so no POL-NS-08 limb fires and no POL-NS-09 escalation trigger is presented. The ruling rests on EV-GPS-02717 and EV-TRIP-02717, both at weight 1.0, so the confidence cap is 1.0. The refund of 4.50 SGD is at or below the 8.00 execution threshold and executes automatically. No clause produces a referral."
